While Jesus was still speaking, someone came up to him and knelt down before him. "My daughter has just died," he said, "but come and put your hand on her, and she will live."
So Jesus got up and went with him, followed by his disciples.
And there was a woman who had been subject to bleeding for twelve years; she came up behind him and touched the fringe of his cloak.
She said to herself, "If I can touch his cloak, I shall be cured."
Then Jesus turned around and saw her. He said to her, "Daughter, your faith has made you well; go in peace." And from that moment the woman was cured.
When Jesus arrived at the house of the synagogue leader, he saw flute players and people wailing loudly.
He said: "Get out! The girl is not dead but asleep." They laughed at him.
When they had put everyone outside, Jesus went in, took the girl by the hand, and she got up.
The news about Jesus spread throughout that whole region. Jesus healed two blind men and cast out an evil spirit.
After Jesus left that place, two blind men followed him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, Son of David!"
When Jesus entered the house, two blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can heal you?" They answered, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes and said, "Let it be done according to your faith."
The two blind men regained their sight. Then Jesus sternly warned them, "Don't tell anyone about what happened."
But they went and spread the news throughout that whole area.
As they were going along, some people brought to him a man who was blind and mute; and they begged him to lay his hand on him.
Jesus cast out the evil spirit, and the mute man began to speak. The people were amazed and said, "Never has anything like this been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees began to say, "He drives out demons by Beelzebul, the prince of demons." 34:9 One of Satan's names. The harvest is plentiful
And Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the gospel of the kingdom. And he healed every disease and every affliction among the people.
When Jesus saw the crowds, he had compassion for them because they were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful but the workers are few."
"They prayed that the Lord of the harvest would send harvesters to the harvest."
Jesus sends his disciples
Jesus called his twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits to cast them out and heal all diseases and sicknesses.
And these are the names of the twelve apostles: first Simon who is also called Peter, his brother Andrew, James son of Zebedee and his brother John,
Phillips and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector, James son of Alphaeus and Thaddeus
Simon who is also called "The Zealot," #TheZealot 4:10 From a Jewish political party that resisted Roman rule, called the Zealots. And Judas Iscariot who betrayed Jesus.
And Jesus sent these twelve and gave them this instruction: "Do not go to Gentile territory or enter a Samaritan town."
But go to the lost sheep of Israel.
They announced that the kingdom of heaven is near.
Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse lepers, cast out demons. You received power without payment; give freely what you received.
Don't carry gold or silver coins in your belts.
Don't bring extra bags or clothes or shoes or crutches. The worker deserves his food.
And when you enter any city or village, look for someone worthy to receive you, and stay with them until you leave that place.
When you enter someone's house, greet their family.
If your family is deserving, then peace will come to them. If they're undeserving, then peace will return to you.
If someone doesn't welcome you in their house or city, and won't listen to your message, then leave that house or that city, and shake off the dust from your feet.
I tell you the truth, it will be more bearable for Sodom and Gomorrah on the day of judgment than for that town. Jesus warns about hardships
I am sending you out like sheep among wolves; therefore be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
Be careful of people because they will hand you over to be tried in their courts and flogged in their synagogues.
They will hand you over to councils and kings for my sake, so that you may bear witness before them. This will be your opportunity to testify about me both to Jews and Gentiles.
When they arrest you, don't worry about what to say because at that moment you will be given the right words to speak
Remember that it is not you who will speak, but your Father's spirit speaking through you.
The brother will hand over his brother to be killed, and the father will hand over his son. And children will turn against their parents and kill them.
All people will hate you because of my name, but whoever remains faithful to the end will be saved.
And when they persecute you in one city, flee to another. I tell you this truth, you will never finish going through all the cities of Israel until the Son of Man comes again.
No student is better than his teacher, nor is a servant greater than his master.
Let the student be content to become like his teacher, and let the servant be content to become like his master. If they call the head of the house Beelzebul, what will they call the rest of its members? Fear God, not people
So don't be afraid of them, for nothing is hidden that will not be revealed, nor anything kept secret that will not be known.
Whatever I tell you in darkness, speak it aloud; whatever I whisper to your ear, proclaim from the rooftops.
Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather, fear him who can destroy both body and soul in hell.
Aren't two birds sold for one coin? However, neither of them falls to the ground without your Father's permission.
But even every hair on your head is numbered.
If you do not fear them, then you are worth more than many sparrows. Confessing Christ before men
Whoever acknowledges me before others, I will also acknowledge before my Father in heaven.
And whoever denies me before men, I will also deny him before my Father who is in heaven. Christ first
Don't think I came to bring peace on earth; I didn't come to give peace but a sword.
I came to say that men will be divided from their fathers, daughters from their mothers, and brides from their mothers-in-law.
So man's enemies will be his own household?
Because anyone who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me; anyone who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever tries to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me; whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Whoever welcomes a prophet because he is a prophet will receive the reward of a prophet. And whoever welcomes someone who is righteous, because they are righteous, will receive the reward of that person.
"And whoever gives one of these little ones just a cup of cold water because he is my disciple, truly I tell you, never will he lose his reward."
John the Baptist or Jesus?
When Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples, he left that place and went to teach and preach in Galilee. Jesus and John the Baptist
While John was in prison, he heard about everything Jesus was doing. So he sent his disciples to ask him
He asked him: "Are you he who is to come, or should we expect another?"
Jesus replied to them, "Go tell John what you have heard and seen."
Here the blind see, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have good news preached to them.
Congratulations to those who do not hesitate in believing me.
When John's disciples left that place, Jesus began to speak to the people about John, saying, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
Why did you go? To see a man in fine clothes? But men who wear fine clothes live in kings' palaces.
So why did you go? To see a prophet? Yes, that's right. But I tell you this: You saw someone greater than a prophet!
This is what it says: "Here I am sending my messenger ahead of you to prepare your way."
I tell you the truth, among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist; yet he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
From John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and forceful men lay hold of it by force.
Because Moses' law and all the prophets prophesied until John.
If you want to accept what prophets and law say, then John is Elijah who was prophesied about his coming. #Elijah...his coming. Malachi 4:11 Elijah was one of God's prophets around 850 BC. Jews were expecting his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
"Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear."
What does this generation resemble? It's like children sitting in markets, calling their friends and saying:
We played for you, but you didn't dance. We sang funeral songs to you, but you didn't cry!
John came, who did not eat like others and did not drink wine like others, so people said about him: "He has an evil spirit."
Then came the Son of Man who ate and drank wine like everyone else, and people said about him: "Look at this man! He's a glutton and drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!" But it is by their fruits that you will recognize them." Jesus warns sinful cities
Then Jesus began to denounce the cities where most of his miracles had been performed, because they hadn't repented of their sins.
He said to them, "Woe unto you, Chorazin! Woe unto you, Bethsaida! For if the mighty works had been done in Tyre and Sidon which have been done in you, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes."
But I tell you that it will be more bearable for Sodom and Gomorrah on Judgment Day than for you.
And you, Capernaum, do you think that you will be lifted up to heaven? No, you will go down to hell! For if the miracles that were done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you that it will be more tolerable for Sodom on the day of judgment than for your people." Jesus is the source of comfort
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children."
Yes, Father, because you were pleased with this work.
The Father has given me everything. No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.
Come to me all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Load Neri #Neri 29:11 The yoke is a wooden tool placed on animals to help them carry heavy loads, it's a Jewish symbol for law. You must follow me, learn from me because I am gentle and humble in heart, so you will find rest for your souls.
"For my yoke is easy to bear, and the burden I give you is light."
Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath; and his disciples were hungry, and began to pluck heads of grain and to eat.
But when the Pharisees saw this, they said to him, "Look! Your disciples are doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath."
Jesus said to them, "Haven't you read in the book of Moses what he did when he was in need and his companions were with him?"
He entered God's house and ate from the bread that was offered to God, although neither he nor those with him were allowed to eat it; this privilege was reserved for priests alone."
Didn't you also read in Moses's law that priests work on Sabbath day and don't keep the Sabbath? Yet they aren't held accountable for this work.
But I tell you that there is something greater than this temple here.
If you knew what it means when the book says: "I want mercy for people, not animal sacrifices." #I want... animal sacrifices. 7:12 From the book of Hosea 6:6 When you judged those innocent ones.
"For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath." Healing on the Sabbath
Then he left that place and went to their gathering.
And there was a man with a shriveled hand. They asked Jesus, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?" They were asking this because they wanted to accuse him.
He said to them, "Suppose one of you has a sheep that falls into a pit on Saturday; won't you go and pull it out?"
And man is more important than sheep. So if you do good deeds on Saturday, it aligns with the law.
Then Jesus said to the man whose hand was paralyzed, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out and his hand became completely well again like the other one.
The Pharisees went out and began to plot how they could kill Jesus. Jesus: God's chosen servant
So Jesus knew what they were planning to do and left that place. Large crowds followed him, and he healed them all.
They were ordered to keep his identity secret.
This happened so that what God said through Isaiah would be fulfilled:
Here is my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights; I will put my spirit upon him, and he shall proclaim justice to the nations.
He won't argue with anyone or shout, and they won't hear his voice in their streets.
It will not break even the bent reed, nor extinguish even the smoking wick. And it will continue until justice triumphs.
And all nations will put their hope in him." The authority of Jesus
Then they brought to him a man who was blind and dumb because he was possessed by an evil spirit; and He healed him, so that the one who had been dumb spoke and saw.
The people were amazed and said, "Could this man be the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow drives out demons by Beelzebul, prince of demons."
And when Jesus knew their thoughts, he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself shall not stand."
If Satan is driving away spirits and fighting himself, how can his kingdom stand?
If I drive out demons by Beelzebul, then what do your disciples use to drive them out? That is how they will judge you.
But if I drive out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
How can one enter the house of a strong man and plunder his possessions unless he first ties up the strong man? Then he can plunder his house.
Whoever is not with me is against me. And whoever does not gather with me scatters.
That's why I tell you this: every sin and insult can be forgiven by people, but insulting the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven.
Whoever speaks against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven, neither in this age nor in the age to come. The fruit reveals the truth.
To get good fruit, plant a good tree. But if you plant an inferior tree, it will give you bad fruit. For by their fruits you shall know them.
You brood of vipers! How can you speak good things when you are evil? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.
A good person produces what is good from their treasure of goodness, while an evil person produces what is evil from their storehouse of wickedness.
But I tell you that on Judgment Day people will be held accountable for every word they have spoken.
"Your words will determine your innocence or guilt." Jewish leaders demand proof
Then some teachers of religious law and Pharisees came up to him with this question: "Teacher, we want you to show us a miraculous sign."
He replied to them, "This wicked generation asks for signs but will be given only the sign of Jonah."
Just as Jonah was in the belly of the great fish for three days and three nights, so will the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth for three days and three nights.
The people of Nineveh will stand against this generation at the judgment day and condemn them because they repented when they heard Jonah's warning. And now here is one greater than Jonah standing in front of you.
And Queen of the South will rise up in judgment with this generation and condemn it, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear Solomon's wisdom; and behold, something greater than Solomon is here. The empty soul
When an unclean spirit goes out of a man, it passes through waterless places seeking rest; and finding none, it says, "I will return to my house from which I came."
Then he says: "I'll go back to my house from where I came." He goes there and finds it empty, swept clean, and tidy.
Then he goes away and brings back seven other spirits more wicked than himself, who go in and live there, and the end of that man is worse than at first.” This generation of evil men is like that. Jesus’ disciples are his family.
While Jesus was speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers came up from behind and stood outside, wanting to speak with him.
One person said to Jesus, "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside, wanting to speak with you."
But he answered them, "Who is my mother? And who are my brothers?"
Then he pointed to his disciples and said: "These are my mother and brothers."
"For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
Like seeds
That day Jesus left home and sat down by the lake.
Large crowds gathered around him. He got in a boat and sat down while the people stood along the shore.
He told them many things in parables. He said to them, "A farmer went out to sow his seed."
And while he was sowing, some seeds fell along the path, and the birds came and ate them up.
Some seeds fell on rocky ground where there was little soil. The plants grew quickly because they didn't have deep roots.
But when the sun came up it burned her, and she withered because she had no roots.
Some seeds fell among thorns. The thorns grew up and choked them.
Other seeds fell on good soil and produced a crop—a hundred times more than was sown in some cases, sixty times more in other cases, and thirty times more in still other cases.
"Whoever has two ears should listen." Hearing and understanding
The disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to them in parables?"
Jesus answered them, "To you it has been given to know the secrets of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it has not been given."
For whoever has will be given more, but whoever does not have, even what they think they have will be taken from them.
That is why I speak to them in parables; though seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear or understand.
And this applies to them what Isaiah prophesied: "You will keep hearing but never understand; you will keep looking but never perceive."
Their minds have become dull, their ears heavy. They've closed their eyes so they can neither see with their eyes nor hear with their ears nor understand with their hearts, for fear that they might return to me and be healed.
But you're lucky because your eyes can see and your ears can hear.
I tell you the truth, many kings and prophets longed to see what you see but didn't see it, and they wanted to hear what you hear but couldn't hear it. The meaning of this parable
Listen to this parable about seeds:
When someone hears the message about God's kingdom but doesn't understand it, the devil comes and takes away what was planted in their heart. This is the meaning of the seeds that fell along the path.
But those that fell on rocky ground were like this: when they heard the word, immediately received it with joy; but because they had no root, they lasted only for a short time. When trouble or persecution came because of the word, they quickly fell away.
But because he has no roots in himself, he stands for a short time, and when trouble and persecution come because of the word that preceded it, he quickly loses his faith.
But those sown among thorns are others who hear the word, yet worldly worries and the deceitfulness of wealth choke it, making it unfruitful.
But he who received seed on good soil is one who hears the word and understands it; who indeed bears fruit, some a hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty." The Parable of Wheat and Tares
He told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field."
But while everyone was sleeping, the enemy came and planted weeds among the wheat, then went away.
When the wheat sprouted and formed ears, so did the weeds.
His servants came to him and said: "Master! Didn't you sow good seeds in your field? Then how did these weeds come up?"
The man replied to them: "My enemy did that." His servants asked him: "Do you want us to go and pull it up?"
The man replied: "No, because when you pull up weeds, sometimes you also pull up wheat."
Let both grow together until harvest time; then I will tell the harvesters, 'First collect the weeds and tie them in bundles to be burned; but gather the wheat into my barn.' " For example, mustard seed and yeast
Then Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that someone took and planted in his field."
It is the smallest of all seeds; but when it grows up, it becomes greater than all herbs and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the air come and lodge in its branches.”
He told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and mixed in with three measures of flour until all of it was leavened."
Jesus used parables to say all these things; in fact, he never spoke to the people without using parables.
This is what God said through the prophet: "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things hidden since the creation of the world."
Then Jesus sent away the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable about the weeds in the field."
He said to them, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man."
The field is the world, and the good seeds are those who belong to the kingdom. But the weeds are those who belong to the evil one.
And the enemy who sowed them is Satan. The harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are angels.
Just like weeds are gathered together and burned by fire, so will be the end of the world.
The Son of Man will send his angels to gather from his kingdom all who cause sin and those who break the law.
Then they throw them in the burning oven. There people cry and gnash their teeth.
Then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun in the kingdom of their Father. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear. For example, treasure and pearls
The kingdom of heaven is like treasure hidden in a field. When someone found it, they hid it again and from their joy went and sold everything they had and bought that field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for beautiful pearls.
When he found a pearl that was extremely valuable, he sold everything he owned to buy it. Like a fishing net
The kingdom of heaven is like a net thrown into the lake that catches fish of every kind.
When the net was full, the fishermen pulled it to shore. Then they sat down and sorted through the fish, keeping the good ones in baskets while throwing away the bad ones.
This is what will happen at the end of the world, when angels will come to separate the wicked from the righteous.
Then they cast the wicked ones into the burning furnace; there they shall weep and gnash their teeth."
Jesus asked his disciples, "Do you understand all these things?" They replied, "Yes."
He said to them, "That is why every teacher of the law who has been instructed about the kingdom of heaven is like the owner of a house who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old." Jesus went back to his hometown.
When Jesus had finished these parables, he left that place.
When he went to his hometown, he began teaching them in their synagogue. And they were amazed and said, "Where did this man get all these things? What kind of wisdom is this that has been given him? How can someone like this do such miracles?"
Isn't he the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Don't all his brothers live among us? Where did he get everything he has?»
But this was an obstacle that prevented them from accepting him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is without honor only in his own country and among his relatives!"
He didn't perform many miracles there because they did not believe in him.
Herod hears about Jesus
At that time Herod the tetrarch heard about everything Jesus was doing and became afraid.
He said to his servants, "It is John the Baptist who has risen from the dead; that's why miraculous powers are at work through him!" The Death of John the Baptist
Herod is the one who arrested John and put him in prison, because of Herodias, his brother Philip's wife.
Because John said to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have your brother's wife."
That's why Herod wanted to kill him, but he was afraid of the people because they considered John a prophet.
But when Herod's birthday came, his daughter danced for him and his guests, and pleased him so much that
He even promised to give her whatever she wanted, no matter what it was.
But her mother had taught her what to say, so she said, "Give me here John the Baptist's head on a platter."
The king was sad but ordered her request to be granted because of his oath and respect for his guests.
He sent soldiers to cut off John's head in prison.
Then he brought his head to her on a plate and she gave it to his mother.
Then his disciples came and took away his body and buried it; and they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds more than five thousand
When Jesus heard this, he got in a boat and went to a secluded place. But when the crowds found out about it, they followed him on foot from all the towns.
When he came down to the shore, he saw a great crowd; and he felt compassion for them and cured their sick.
In the evening, his disciples came to him saying, "This place is desolate and it's already late; send them away so they can go to the villages and buy food for themselves."
But Jesus said to them, "They don't need to go away; you give them something to eat."
His disciples said to him, "We have here only five loaves of bread and two fish."
Jesus said to them, "Bring her here."
He ordered them to sit down in groups on the grass. Then Jesus took the five loaves and two fish, looked up towards heaven and gave thanks over them. He broke the loaves in pieces, gave them to his disciples who distributed them among the people.
They all ate until they were full, and when they picked up the leftovers there were twelve baskets of broken pieces left over.
There were about five thousand men who had eaten, besides women and children. Jesus walked on the water
Then Jesus asked his disciples to get in the boat and go ahead of him to the other side, while he dismissed the crowd.
After sending them away, he went up to the mountain by himself to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was in the middle of the lake, with waves crashing against it forcefully because the wind was blowing against its direction.
And just before dawn, Jesus came to them walking on the lake.
When his disciples saw him walking on the lake, they were terrified and thought it was a ghost, and they cried out in fear.
Jesus immediately said to them, "Take courage! It is I. Don't be afraid."
Peter answered him, "Lord, if it's really you, tell me to come to you on the water."
He said to him, "Come." So Peter got down out of the boat and walked on water and came toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw that the wind was strong, he became frightened and began to sink; so he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Then Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught him, saying to him, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?"
When Jesus got in the boat with Peter, the wind stopped blowing.
Those who were in the boat worshiped him, saying, "Truly you are the Son of God." Jesus healed many people.
When they crossed the lake, they came to the region of Gennesaret.
When people in that area recognized Jesus, they spread news about his arrival throughout the surrounding region. They brought all their sick to him.
They begged him to let them just touch his clothes, and everyone who touched it was healed.
God's law and people's traditions
Then some Pharisees and teachers of religious law came to Jesus from Jerusalem and asked him,
Why do your students break traditions that we inherited from our ancestors? They don't even wash their hands before eating.
Jesus replied to them, "Why do you break God's commandment because of your tradition?"
God commanded and said: "Honor your father and mother." #Honor ... your mother. 4:15 From Exodus 20:12 and Deuteronomy 5:16. He also said: "Whoever curses his father or mother shall be put to death." #Whoever curses...shall be put to death. 4:15 From Exodus 21:17.
But you say, 'If someone says to his father or mother, "Whatever help you might have needed from me is dedicated to God," he is exempt from honoring his father or mother.'
And by doing this you have ignored God's commandment because of your traditions.
O hypocrites! Believe Isaiah when he prophesied about you:
This people honors me with their lips, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship is useless because they teach commandments that are nothing but human decrees.
Jesus called his disciples to him and said to them, "Listen carefully to what I am about to tell you."
"It is what comes out of a person that defiles them, not what goes into their mouth."
Then his disciples came to him and said, "Do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard what you said?"
Jesus replied, "Every plant that my heavenly Father has not planted will be pulled up by its roots."
Leave them alone; they're blind leading the blind. If one blind man leads another, both will fall into the pit."
Peter replied, "Explain this parable to us."
Then Jesus said to them, "Don't you understand yet?"
Don't you understand that whatever goes into a person's mouth ends up in the stomach and then leaves the body?
But what comes out of a person's mouth proceeds from the heart, this is what defiles them.
For from within, out of a person's heart, come evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, greed, malice, deceit, lewdness, envy, slander, arrogance and folly.
These are the things that make a person unclean, but eating with unwashed hands does not make one unclean." Jesus helps an outsider woman
Jesus left that place and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman from that vicinity came to him, crying out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is suffering terribly from demon-possession."
But Jesus did not answer her a word. Then his disciples came to him and begged him, saying, "Send her away, for she keeps crying out after us."
He said: "I have come to seek only the lost sheep of Israel."
But the woman came up to him and knelt down before him, saying, "Lord, help me."
Jesus answered her, "It is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to their dogs."
She said, "That's true, sir, but even dogs eat what falls from their masters' table."
Then Jesus answered her, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you according to your desire." And at that moment her daughter was cured. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus left that place and went to an area near Lake Galilee. He climbed up a hill and sat down there.
Large crowds came to him, bringing with them lame, blind, crippled, mute, and many other sick people who laid them down at Jesus' feet; he healed them.
The crowds were amazed when they saw the deaf speaking, the lame walking, the paralyzed moving, and the blind seeing - so they praised God of Israel. Jesus fed more than four thousand people.
Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may collapse on the way."
His disciples said to him, "Where can we get enough bread in this deserted place to feed such a crowd?"
Jesus asked them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said, "Seven loaves and a few small fish."
Jesus ordered the people to sit down on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and the fish, gave thanks, broke them up, and handed them to his disciples who distributed them among the people.
They all ate until they were full. Then they gathered up what was left over - seven baskets full.
And there were four thousand men who ate, besides women and children.
When Jesus sent away the crowds, he got into a boat and went to the region of Magdala.
Jewish leaders test Jesus
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to Jesus to test him, asking for a sign from God to prove that he was endorsed by Him.
Jesus answered them, "When it is evening you say, 'It will be fair weather, for the sky is red.'
In the early morning you say: "It will be stormy today because the sky is red and overcast." You know how to interpret signs in nature, but you don't understand the times you live in!
This wicked generation seeks for signs but will be given none except the sign of Jonah." Then he left them and went away. Lack of understanding
Jesus's disciples crossed to the other side of the lake, but they forgot to bring bread with them.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out! Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the children began to talk among themselves: "But we haven't brought bread!"
Jesus knew what they were saying, so he said to them, "Why do you discuss among yourselves that you have no bread?
Haven't you realized yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand, how many baskets full of leftovers did you collect?
Don't you remember the seven baskets full of leftover bread from feeding four thousand people?
Why don't you understand that I was talking about something other than ordinary bread? I was warning you to beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then his disciples understood that he was not speaking about bread but about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter declares that Jesus is the Christ
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that I am?"
His disciples answered him, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; still others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
He said to them, "And you, who do you say I am?"
Then Simon Peter answered him, "Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus replied, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, because flesh and blood did not reveal this to you, but my Father in heaven."
And I tell you that you are Peter, #Peter. 18:16 From Greek "petros" meaning "rock." And upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell #gates of hell. 18:16 Meaning power of death. Will not be able to overcome it.
And I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven; whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."
Then he strictly ordered his disciples to tell no one that he was Jesus. Jesus speaks about the certainty of his death
From that time on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised.
But Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "God forbid it, Lord! This shall never happen to you."
Then Jesus turned to Peter and said, "Get behind me, Satan! You're a stumbling block to me; you don't have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come with me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me."
Whoever wants to save his life will lose it; but whoever loses his life for me will find it.
What good is it for someone to gain the whole world yet forfeit their soul? Or what can anyone give in exchange for their soul?
For the Son of Man is going to come in his Father's glory with his angels, and then he will reward each person according to what they have done.
I tell you the truth, some who are standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”
Jesus with Moses and Elijah
After six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain apart by themselves.
While they were there, Jesus was transfigured before them. His face shone like the sun, and his clothes became as bright as a flash of lightning.
Suddenly Moses and Elijah appeared before them talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Master, it is good for us to be here. Let us put up three shelters—one for you, one for Moses and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them; and a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him."
When they heard this, the disciples were terrified and fell down to worship him.
Then Jesus approached them and touched them, saying, "Get up! Don't be afraid."
When they looked around, they saw no one but Jesus.
As they were going down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, "Don't tell anyone what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
His disciples asked him, "Why do teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?" #Elijah... first. 10:17 Elijah was one of God's prophets around 850 BC. The Jews expected his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
Jesus replied to them, "Yes, Elijah is coming first to restore everything."
But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and they didn't recognize him; but did to him everything they wished. Likewise the Son of Man will also suffer at their hands."
Then his disciples understood that he was speaking about John the Baptist. Jesus casts an evil spirit out of a boy
When they came back to the crowd, a man approached Jesus and knelt before him
He said: "Have mercy on my son, Lord, he suffers from epilepsy and is in great pain. He often falls into fire or water."
And I brought him to your disciples, but they couldn't heal him."
Jesus said to them, "How long shall I stay with you? How long must I put up with you?" And he called for the boy.
Jesus ordered the evil spirit to leave him, and instantly the boy was cured.
Then his disciples came to him privately and said, "Why couldn't we drive it out?"
Jesus replied, "Because of your little faith. Truly I tell you, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
But this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting." Jesus foretells his approaching death.
As they were traveling through Galilee together, Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is about to be handed over to human authorities."
And they will kill him. But on the third day he will rise from the dead.” So his disciples were greatly distressed. The Temple Tax
When Jesus came to Capernaum, those who collected taxes from him approached Peter and said, "Doesn't your teacher pay temple tax?"
Peter answered them, "Yes." Then Peter went home. Jesus met him at the door and said, "Simon, tell me, from whom do earthly kings collect taxes or poll-tax? From their own children or from foreigners?"
Peter replied, "They collect it from other nations." Jesus said, "Then the children are exempt from it."
But so that we don't cause them trouble, go to the lake, cast your fishing line. Then take the first fish you catch, open its mouth. You will find inside it a coin worth four dirhams. Take it and give it to them for me and yourself."
